Each time the ball bounces, it reaches a height that is $\frac{2}{3}$ of the previous height. We start with a height of $243$ cm after the first drop. After the first bounce, the height is \[
243 \times \frac{2}{3} = 162\ \text{cm.}
After the second bounce, the height becomes \[
162 \times \frac{2}{3} = 108\ \text{cm.}
After the third bounce, the height becomes \[
108 \times \frac{2}{3} = 72\ \text{cm.}
After the fourth bounce, the height becomes \[
72 \times \frac{2}{3} = 48\ \text{cm.}
After the fifth bounce, the height becomes \[
48 \times \frac{2}{3} = 32\ \text{cm.}
After the sixth bounce, the height becomes \[
32 \times \frac{2}{3} \approx 21.33\ \text{cm.}
Since we want to find the first bounce where the ball rises less than $30$ cm, we see that after the sixth bounce, the height is still greater than $30$ cm. After the seventh bounce, the height becomes \[
21.33 \times \frac{2}{3} \approx 14.22\ \text{cm.}
Thus, the ball first rises less than $30$ cm after $\boxed{7}$ bounces.

We are given a club consisting of $11$ men and $12$ women and we need to choose a committee such that the number of women on the committee is one more than the number of men on the committee. We can represent the number of men on the committee as $m$ and the number of women on the committee as $w$. The condition that the number of women on the committee is one more than the number of men on the committee can be written as \[w = m+1.\] There are $11$ men to choose from and $12$ women to choose from. For each possible value of $m$, there are $\binom{11}{m}$ ways to choose the men and $\binom{12}{m+1}$ ways to choose the women. Therefore, the total number of committees that can be formed is given by
\begin{align*}
N &= \sum_{m=0}^{11} \binom{11}{m} \binom{12}{m+1}
\end{align*}We can then simplify this expression using some binomial identity:
\begin{align*}
N &= \sum_{m=0}^{11} \binom{11}{m} \binom{12}{12-m} \\
&= \sum_{m=0}^{11} \binom{11}{m} \binom{11}{12-m-m} \\
&= \sum_{m=0}^{11} \binom{11}{m} \binom{11}{22-2m} \\
&= \binom{22}{22}
\end{align*}The probability that a randomly chosen committee of $22$ members is correctly formed is given by \[\frac{\binom{22}{22}}{\binom{23}{22}} = \frac{1}{23}.\]Therefore, the probability that a randomly chosen committee of $22$ members is incorrectly formed is \[1 - \frac{1}{23} = \boxed{22}.\]

